speechtext:
1.	In each of the years 1941 to 1947 inclusive, what has been (i) the average price per one hundred pounds paid in sugar control or by the sugar controller for raw sugars, f.o.b. (a) Cuba; (b) San Domingo; (c) British West Indies; (d) other British possessions? (ii) The laid-down cost of such sugars at the ports of Saint John, Montreal and Vancouver?
2.	What was the freight per one hundred pounds in each of said years from (a) Cuba;
(b)	San Domingo; (c) British West Indies; (d) other British possessions, to each of said ports?
3.	What price was paid per one hundred pounds for raw sugars f.o.b. from each of the following sources; (a) Cuba; (b) San Domingo;
(c)	British West Indies; (d) other British possessions, for the fourth quarter of the year 1946?
4.	Will the prices paid during the fourth quarter of 1946 for Cuban and San Dominigan raw sugars be the minimum prices for sugars from their respective 1947 crops. If the prices have not been settled yet, what is it estimated the prices for 1947 will be?
